  "description": "Tokens, the assurance cookie, the assurance policy and action tokens. TEST KEYS ONLY: signing_keys are Ed25519 seeds (base64url, 32 bytes) that exist for these vectors; never use them anywhere else. A token case gives either a literal \"token\" or \"claims\" to sign: the base claims of that kind, with \"set\" fields replaced and \"remove\" fields deleted, signed by \"sign_with\" (default: the key its kid names). \"token_transform\" then edits the signed token (\"pad_payload\", \"respell_signature\" (the same 64 bytes, a different spelling), \"extra_part\", \"drop_signature\", \"version_v2\", \"swap_payload_from:<case name>\"). Times are seconds; \"now\" is the verifier's clock. \"snapshot\" is a format-1 snapshot (section 5) whose keys are the public halves of k1 and k2; \"other\" is a key the snapshot does not hold. Assurance cases run check(policy, request, { target }) for the app \"app\" on \"hostname\", with the member from the identity headers resolved against \"snapshot\" and \"administrators\" as the members isAdministrator accepts. A case's \"cookie\" is the Cookie header, where \"<token>\" is replaced by the signed token. \"action\" goes in X-Sovereign-Action-Token. In action claims \"dig\": \"<digest>\" means the digest of the case's own request and target. \"redeem\" is what Home Base answers (redeemed, replayed, not_authorized, invalid, unavailable); \"redeem_calls\" is how many times the app may ask.",
